<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<display version="2.0.0">
  <name>BLM Interlock</name>
  <width>900</width>
  <height>700</height>
  <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
    <name>Title</name>
    <text>Interlock status and settings</text>
    <x>15</x>
    <y>12</y>
    <width>260</width>
    <height>30</height>
    <font>
      <font name="Header 2" family="Liberation Sans" style="BOLD" size="18.0">
      </font>
    </font>
  </widget>
  <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
    <name>SettingsTitle</name>
    <text>Interlock settings</text>
    <x>15</x>
    <y>50</y>
    <width>300</width>
    <height>25</height>
    <font>
      <font name="Header 3" family="Liberation Sans" style="BOLD" size="16.0">
      </font>
    </font>
  </widget>
  <widget type="bool_button" version="2.0.0">
    <name>Enable</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):ILK:EN</pv_name>
    <x>15</x>
    <y>80</y>
    <width>125</width>
    <height>32</height>
    <tooltip>$(P):ILK:EN (ILK_EN)

Master enable of the interlock output. While it is off, the FPGA keeps
evaluating the thresholds and the status bits keep updating, but no interlock
is asserted downstream.</tooltip>
  </widget>
  <widget type="action_button" version="3.0.0">
    <name>Reset</name>
    <actions>
      <action type="write_pv">
        <description>RESET</description>
        <pv_name>$(pv_name)</pv_name>
        <value>1</value>
      </action>
    </actions>
    <pv_name>$(P):ILK:RST</pv_name>
    <text>Reset</text>
    <x>150</x>
    <y>80</y>
    <width>125</width>
    <height>32</height>
    <tooltip>$(actions)

Clears the latched interlock. Writing 1 generates a reset pulse in the FPGA,
which releases the interlock output and lets the history BRAMs capture the
next event.</tooltip>
  </widget>
  <widget type="combo" version="2.0.0">
    <name>Polarity</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):ILK:POL</pv_name>
    <x>285</x>
    <y>80</y>
    <width>115</width>
    <height>32</height>
    <tooltip>$(P):ILK:POL (ILK_POL)

Polarity of the interlock output:
  Positive - the interlock output is not inverted.
  Negative - the interlock output is inverted.

It has to match what the downstream machine protection input expects. The
driver writes the single polarity bit straight to the FPGA when the selection
changes.</tooltip>
    <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    <items>
      <item>Positive</item>
      <item>Negative</item>
    </items>
  </widget>
  <widget type="textentry" version="3.0.0">
    <name>Number</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):ILK:NUM</pv_name>
    <x>410</x>
    <y>80</y>
    <width>95</width>
    <height>32</height>
    <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    <tooltip>$(P):ILK:NUM (ILK_NUM)

Interlock number reported to the FPGA interlock block, which uses it to tag
the event in the history BRAM and in the interlock status word.</tooltip>
  </widget>
  <widget type="textentry" version="3.0.0">
    <name>Filter</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):ILK:FILT_NUM</pv_name>
    <x>515</x>
    <y>80</y>
    <width>95</width>
    <height>32</height>
    <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    <tooltip>$(P):ILK:FILT_NUM (ILK_FILT_NUM)

Number of consecutive samples that have to exceed a threshold before the
interlock is declared. Raising it makes the interlock less sensitive to
single-sample spikes, at the cost of a slower reaction. Default 1.</tooltip>
  </widget>
  <widget type="textentry" version="3.0.0">
    <name>Hold</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):ILK:HOLD_TIME</pv_name>
    <x>620</x>
    <y>80</y>
    <width>95</width>
    <height>32</height>
    <format>3</format>
    <precision>6</precision>
    <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    <tooltip>$(P):ILK:HOLD_TIME (ILK_HOLD_TIME)

Time the interlock output stays asserted after the condition clears, so the
downstream system reliably sees the event even for a very short loss spike.
Default 5000.</tooltip>
  </widget>
  <widget type="textentry" version="3.0.0">
    <name>Latch</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):ILK:LATCH_LEN</pv_name>
    <x>725</x>
    <y>80</y>
    <width>120</width>
    <height>32</height>
    <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    <tooltip>$(P):ILK:LATCH_LEN (ILK_LATCH_LEN)

Number of 10 ms samples the history BRAM keeps around an interlock event. The
IOC waits for this many samples plus 100 ms, then reads and reorders the points
around the lock point to build the interlock history waveforms. Default 2048
(20.58 s wait).</tooltip>
  </widget>
